<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Periodcentered on Typophile Archive</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/periodcentered/</link><description>Recent content in Periodcentered on Typophile Archive</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 28 Dec 2007 13:18:00 +0100</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/periodcentered/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Dot</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/40388/</link><pubDate>Fri, 28 Dec 2007 13:18:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/40388/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Small circular character used as a universal typographic element, principally in the role of a &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20131019005803/http:/typophile.com/wiki/period"&gt;period&lt;/a&gt;, but also as part of an &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20131019005803/http:/typophile.com/wiki/i"&gt;i&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20131019005803/http:/typophile.com/wiki/j"&gt;j&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20131019005803/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Comma"&gt;commas&lt;/a&gt; are often extensions of the period dot.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>